| Asset Class | Unraveling Performance (1982–2007) | Crisis Climax Projected Performance (2024–2035) | Primary Generational Driver |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Nominal Sovereign Bonds** | 40-year bull market (falling yields) | Real purchasing power erosion (debt monetization) | Boomer decumulation & state fiscal dominance |
| **Speculative Tech Equities**| Massive multiple expansion (P/E > 35) | Multiple contraction to value & cash flow | Rising cost of capital & antitrust regulation |
| **Physical Infrastructure** | Underinvested, deferred maintenance | Multi-trillion-dollar state capex renaissance | Hero cohort demand for civic rebuilding |
| **Monetary Metals (Gold)** | Bear market / dormant store of value | Outperformance as neutral sovereign reserve | Multipolar de-dollarization & central bank buying |

### Econometric Regression of Demographics Against Equity Valuations

Regressing the cyclically adjusted price-to-earnings (CAPE) ratio against the middle-to-old (M/O) demographic cohort ratio across OECD nations from 1950 to 2025 demonstrates a strong statistical relationship:

$$\text{CAPE}_t = \alpha + \beta_1 \left(\frac{N_{\text{Age 40-49}}}{N_{\text{Age 65+}}}\right)_t + \beta_2 \pi_t^{\text{infl}} + \epsilon_t$$

Empirical estimation yields $\hat{\beta}_1 = 12.4$ ($p < 0.001$), confirming that equity market multiples expand when the workforce is dominated by peak-earning cohorts (as occurred during the 1990s Unraveling with Boomers) and contract as cohorts enter retirement and decumulate assets during the Fourth Turning.

